    title: "Summer 2024, Unit 1, Question 9",
    md: "Only a minority gained full marks. A large group did not realise they had to differentiate at all and set the line equal to the curve instead. Others misread the information and used the given line's gradient where the perpendicular one belonged, so the normal in the last part was wrong even though the arithmetic was sound.",
    source: "ccea-cer:further-maths:2024-summer:FM1:Q9",
  },
  {
    type: "callout",
    kind: "examiner",
    title: "Summer 2025, Unit 1, Question 13",
    md: "In part (i) a frequent mistake was solving $\\frac{dy}{dx} = 0$ rather than setting the derivative equal to the stated gradient, which the report puts down to habit. In part (ii) most found the perpendicular gradient correctly, then stopped there, reused the earlier part's values, or wrote a line equation where coordinates had been asked for.",
    source: "ccea-cer:further-maths:2025-summer:FM1:Q13",
  },
  {
    type: "callout",
    kind: "examiner",
    title: "Summer 2023, Unit 1, Question 10 and Summer 2022, Unit 1, Question 8",
    md: "In 2023 many earned full marks on a normal, and the losses were arithmetic: the gradient at $x = -1$, or the constant in the finished equation. In 2022 part (ii) separated the stronger candidates, and the examiners were surprised how few managed the first step of writing down the gradient the question had already given them.",
    source: "ccea-cer:further-maths:2023-summer:FM1:Q10",
  },
  { type: "prompt", promptId: id("rp", "01") },
